auditcore_identifiers 0.2.0 — attribution and authorization

New implementation in auditcore. The strict profile follows ISO 13616 (IBAN),
ISO 9362 (BIC), ISO 17442 (LEI), ISO/IEC 7064 (MOD 97-10, MOD 11,10), the VAT
number formats of the EU member states and the German tax identifier rules.
The legacy profiles reproduce the executed behaviour of
janpow77/flowinvoice (commit fb2d18568d2eaf64574d131ceae51a936b9aac02:
backend/app/services/validators.py, backend/app/pipeline/stages/validation.py,
backend/app/services/risk_checker.py), janpow77/audit-portal (commit
d8eefa426826bdecb67036774f3128ae05e7d0d0, same files), janpow77/flowworkshop
(commit a05bb2143bd96d5e981f9462f05b965e1658be36: entity_resolution.py) and
of the auditcore-internal copies (auditcore_invoicesynth, auditcore_documents,
auditcore_entity_matching at commit 99788a18c28bf683ada62bb3ab9d4aeb36f2c5b2).
Patterns and messages were adopted; the code was rewritten. Blob SHAs are
listed in provenance.json.

On 2026-09-22 the rights holder decided: "die Bibliotheken sollen MIT sein,
die anderen Repos nicht; die Bibliotheken kommen nach auditcore". Status:
USER_AUTHORIZED_MIT for this library only; the applications keep their own
licensing.

Reference data (facts): BBAN structures of the SWIFT IBAN Registry release
101 as transcribed by python-stdnum 2.2 (stdnum/iban.dat), ISO 3166-1 codes
from Debian iso-codes 4.16.0. python-stdnum (LGPL-2.1+) is used only by the
offline tool tools/crosscheck_stdnum.py; no stdnum code is included and the
library has no runtime dependency.
